    <h1>Chrome Extension Privacy Policy: What Google Requires and How to Create One</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published August 26, 2026 &middot; Updated September 10, 2026</p>

    <p>Every Chrome extension that handles user data needs a privacy policy URL before Google will approve it for the Chrome Web Store. The policy must be publicly accessible, describe exactly what data the extension collects and why, and match the data-use disclosures you certify in the Developer Dashboard's Privacy practices tab. Skip it and the extension fails review. Submit a vague or generic document and the review team rejects it anyway -- they read the policy, not just the URL field, and they cross-check it against the "Single purpose" description and permission justifications you filed alongside it.</p>

    <p>This guide covers when the Chrome Web Store requires a policy, what Google's review process actually checks, how to fix the most common rejection emails, the data categories extension developers commonly overlook, how the "Single purpose" field and the Limited Use policy interact with your privacy policy, how to write permission justifications that pass review, how to complete the Data Usage certification, where to host the finished policy, and a complete sample policy you can copy. If you need a policy fast, you can <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate a privacy policy for free</a> and host it at no cost.</p>

